WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 191: Ronald Reagan: Tear Down This Wall, Reykjavik, and the Cold War (1985–1989)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.920
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our journey with Ronald Reagan.

4
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:20.880
[Rebecca] In the final years of the 20th century, Reagan combined moral clarity, military pressure, and daring personal diplomacy to bring about the peaceful collapse of Soviet communism.

5
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:29.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Reykjavik summit, the Berlin Wall, and the end of the Cold War.

6
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:43.360
[George] In March 1983, speaking to the National Association of Evangelicals in Orlando, Florida, Ronald Reagan dropped a rhetorical thunderbolt into the Cold War, rejecting the moral equivalence of détente.

7
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:53.040
[George] Reagan urged Americans not to ignore the aggressive impulses of an evil empire and labeled Soviet communism the focus of evil in the modern world.

8
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:59.840
[George] Foreign policy elites in Washington and Europe gasped in horror, accusing Reagan of reckless warmongering.

9
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:10.880
[George] Two weeks later, on March 23, Reagan unveiled his most audacious concept, the Strategic Defense Initiative, SDI nicknamed Star Wars by critics.

10
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:21.280
[George] Reagan proposed using cutting-edge lasers, satellites, and particle beams to intercept and destroy ballistic missiles in flight, rendering nuclear weapons obsolete.

11
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] Wouldn't it be better to save lives than to avenge them?

12
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.760
[George] Reagan asked.

13
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:33.600
[George] The Soviet Politburo was terrified, already economically exhausted by their war in Afghanistan and domestic stagnation.

14
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:54.320
[George] Reagan's bold moral indictment of Soviet totalitarianism injected a powerful ideological clarity into the Cold War, rejecting the defeatist notion that the West had to accommodate tyranny indefinitely.

15
00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:03.800
[George] By challenging the legitimacy of the Soviet system, Reagan altered the diplomatic dynamic, putting totalitarianism on the defensive across the globe.

16
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:14.120
[George] For years, Reagan had joked about his inability to negotiate with Soviet leaders, remarking, How am I supposed to get any place with the Russians if they keep dying on me?

17
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.160
[George] Referring to the rapid succession of Brezhnev, Andropov, and Chernenko.

18
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:27.320
[George] But in March 1985, 54-year-old Mikhail Sergeevich Gorbashev became general secretary of the Communist Party.

19
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:39.000
[George] Young, educated and realistic, Gorbashev recognized that Soviet communism was rotting from within and desperately needed Glosnost, openness and perestroika, restructuring.

20
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.320
[George] In November 1985, Reagan and Gorbachev held their first summit in Geneva, Switzerland.

21
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:55.960
[George] Meeting at Fleur d'O, a picturesque 19th-century villa on Lake Geneva, Reagan suggested they walk down to a small boathouse where a cozy fire was crackling.

22
00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:04.360
[George] Sitting alone by the fireplace with only translators, the two Titans discovered an unexpected, profound personal chemistry.

23
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:10.680
[George] Though they argued fiercely about human rights and SDI, they agreed on an immortal joint declaration.

24
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.360
[George] A nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.

25
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.960
[George] The psychological ice of the Cold War had begun to thaw.

26
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:29.240
[George] The genuine personal rapport established by the lakeside fireplace in Geneva proved that two determined leaders could transcend decades of diplomatic hatred through authentic human respect.

27
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:39.560
[George] The fireside summit proved that personal diplomacy, mutual honesty, and genuine human engagement can melt even the most entrenched geopolitical hostility.

28
00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:53.120
[George] In October 1986, Reagan and Gorbyshev met for an impromptu weekend summit in Reykjavik, Iceland, inside Hofdi House, a haunted, solitary wooden mansion overlooking the stormy North Atlantic.

29
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:59.840
[George] What was planned as a preliminary meeting erupted into the most extraordinary disarmament conference in human history.

30
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:12.720
[George] Sitting across a small bayes table, Gorbachev made stunning concession after concession, proposing 50% cuts in strategic weapons and the total elimination of all intermediate range missiles in Europe.

31
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.600
[George] Then Reagan leaned in and raised the stakes.

32
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.680
[George] Why not eliminate all nuclear ballistic missiles within ten years?

33
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.720
[George] Gorbachev replied, we can agree to that.

34
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.120
[George] For several breathless hours, the two leaders stood on the threshold of abolishing nuclear weapons entirely.

35
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.800
[George] Then came the impasse.

36
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.640
[George] Gorbachev demanded that SDI testing be confined strictly to the laboratory for ten years.

37
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:42.080
[George] Reagan refused to give up the defensive shield that he believed would protect future generations.

38
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:49.360
[George] The summit collapsed, photographs captured the two men parting in the cold drizzle, visibly sombre and exhausted.

39
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:58.080
[George] Critics lamented failure, but Reykjavik broke the conceptual deadlock, laying the groundwork for the most sweeping arms reduction treaties in history.

40
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:08.720
[George] In Reykjavik, the two leaders dared to imagine a world entirely free of nuclear terror, breaking the psychological paralysis of mutual assured destruction forever.

41
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:18.080
[George] The discussions at Hofdi House pushed the boundaries of what was conceivable in arms control, inspiring future generations of disarmament negotiators.

42
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:25.760
[George] Just three weeks after Reykjavik, Reagan was engulfed by the worst crisis of his presidency, the Iran-Contra affair.

43
00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:43.480
[George] On November 3, 1986, a Lebanese magazine revealed that the United States had been secretly selling antitank and anti-aircraft missiles to the radical Islamic regime of Ayatollah Khomeini in Iran in exchange for the release of American hostages held in Lebanon by Hezbollah.

44
00:05:43.480 --> 00:06:00.280
[George] Worse, Attorney General Edwin Meese discovered that National Security Council aide Lieutenant Colonel Oliver North had secretly diverted $12 million in profits from the Iranian armed sales to fund the Contra rebels fighting the Marxist Sandinista government in Nicaragua.

45
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:11.720
[George] In direct violation of the Boland Amendment passed by Congress, Reagan's public approval rating cratered by 21 points in a single month, the largest drop in polling history.

46
00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:22.280
[George] On March 4, 1987, after an independent tower commission faulted his detached management style, Reagan delivered a remarkable, humble television address.

47
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:27.160
[George] A few months ago I told the American people I did not trade arms for hostages.

48
00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] My heart and my best intentions still tell me that's true, but the facts and the evidence tell me it is not.

49
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:41.080
[George] By confronting the truth, accepting blame, and replacing his staff, Reagan slowly regained public trust.

50
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] Reagan's candid, humble televised address regarding Arancontra showed a president willing to confront painful operational failures directly, restoring trust through transparency and accountability.

51
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:02.840
[George] In taking responsibility for the errors of his administration, Reagan upheld the vital principle that ultimate accountability rests with the commander in chief.

52
00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:11.320
[George] On June 12, 1987, President Reagan traveled to West Berlin to commemorate the city's 750th anniversary.

53
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:21.680
[George] Standing on a platform behind bulletproof glass at the Brandenburg Gate, Reagan looked across the concrete wall crowned with barbed wire and guarded by East German watchtowers.

54
00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:38.720
[George] His speechwriter, Peter Robinson, had drafted a bold sentence demanding the destruction of the wall, but both the State Department and National Security Advisor Colin Powell repeatedly struck the line from the draft, warning that it was too provocative and would humiliate Gorbachev.

55
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.160
[George] In the presidential limousine on the way to the speech, Reagan turned to his aides and smiled.

56
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.800
[George] The boys at state are going to kill me.

57
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.960
[George] But it's the right thing to do.

58
00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:57.600
[George] Facing an audience of 20,000 West Berliners and radio microphones projecting into East Germany, Reagan looked toward the wall and thundered.

59
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:08.160
[George] If you seek prosperity for the Soviet Union and Eastern Europe, if you seek liberalization, come here to this gate.

60
00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:08.480
[George] Mr.

61
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.560
[George] Gorbachev, open this gate.

62
00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:10.960
[George] Mr.

63
00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.360
[George] Gorbachev, tear down this wall.

64
00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.640
[George] Two years later, the wall came tumbling down.

65
00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:27.760
[George] That ringing challenge at the Brandenburg Gate captured the moral yearning of millions held captive behind the Iron Curtain, proving that words of freedom are more powerful than concrete walls.

66
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] The historic oration in West Berlin stood as a ringing declaration that the human desire for liberty is universal and will eventually tear down any wall built to contain it.

67
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:44.640
[George] In December 1987, Mikhail Gorbachev traveled to Washington for a historic state summit.

68
00:08:44.640 --> 00:09:01.400
[George] In the East Room of the White House, Reagan and Gorbashev signed the Intermediate Range Nuclear Forces Treaty, eliminating an entire category of nuclear weapons, 2,692 missiles, with unprecedented on-site inspection protocols.

69
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.840
[George] At the signing ceremony, Reagan quoted a Russian proverb that made Gorbachev laugh aloud.

70
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:10.920
[George] Duvriy, no provery, trust, but verify.

71
00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.960
[George] In May 1988, Reagan completed his journey by traveling to Moscow.

72
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:25.320
[George] Walking through Red Square bathed in sunlight, surrounded by crowds of smiling Russian citizens, Reagan draped an arm around Gorbachev's shoulders.

73
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.400
[George] A reporter shouted, Mr.

74
00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.880
[George] President, do you still think the Soviet Union is an evil empire?

75
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:38.200
[George] Reagan shook his head gently and replied, No, I was talking about another time, another era.

76
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.840
[George] The ideological crusade had triumphed.

77
00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:47.800
[George] Without firing a single missile, the Cold War had been won through strength, patience, and human trust.

78
00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:59.400
[George] Walking through Red Square bathed in spring sunlight, Reagan demonstrated that unwavering strength paired with diplomatic generosity could bring about the peaceful conclusion of a four-decade conflict.

79
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:09.400
[George] The walk through Red Square symbolized the end of the Cold War, proving that ideological divides can be healed through patience, strength, and mutual respect.

80
00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] On January 11, 1989, Ronald Reagan delivered his farewell address from the Oval Office, sharing his enduring vision of America.

81
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.200
[George] I've spoken of the shining city all my political life.

82
00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:28.200
[George] In my mind it was a tall, proud city built on rocks stronger than oceans, windswept.

83
00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.160
[George] God blessed, and teeming with people of all kinds living in harmony and peace.

84
00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:44.440
[George] In November 1994, five years after leaving office, Reagan wrote a heartbreaking, handwritten letter to the American people announcing that he had been diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease.

85
00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.720
[George] I now begin the journey that will lead me into the sunset of my life.

86
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:52.560
[George] I know that for America there will always be a bright dawn ahead.

87
00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.320
[George] Thank you, my friends.

88
00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.480
[George] May God always bless you.

89
00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:06.240
[George] For ten quiet years, shielded by Nancy's devoted love, he slipped away into memory, passing away in June 2004 at the age of 93.

90
00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:17.440
[George] Ronald Reagan left behind a transformed world, having conquered economic doubt, revived the American spirit, and liberated millions from the grip of totalitarian tyranny.

91
00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:28.560
[George] His final handwritten letter announcing his Alzheimer's diagnosis was a masterclass in personal grace, offering comfort to the nation even as he prepared to enter the twilight of memory.

92
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:38.480
[George] Ronald Reagan's shining city upon a hill remains an enduring beacon of hope, reminding the world that freedom is the sacred birthright of all mankind.

93
00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.080
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the presidency of George H.

94
00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:00.480
[Rebecca] W.

95
00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:11.360
[Rebecca] Bush, being shot down over the Pacific at age 20, the collapse of the Berlin Wall, the triumph of Operation Desert Storm, and the famous White House ban on broccoli.

96
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.760
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

